TAKEAWAYS = {
    # ---- Chapter 1 ----
    '1:1': "The sayings come with a name and a throne attached. This is not anonymous folk wisdom but a king's collection.",
    '1:2': "The book states its purpose before it begins, which is a way of admitting that proverbs can be misused.",
    '1:3': "Wisdom is unpacked as justice, judgment and equity — how a man deals with others, not how clever he is alone.",
    '1:4': "\"Subtilty to the simple.\" The shrewdness that makes a con man is here handed to the naive as a defence.",
    '1:5': "The wise man is the one still listening. Wisdom is measured by appetite for more of it, never by arrival.",
    '1:6': "\"Dark sayings.\" The book expects to be worked at; a proverb understood too quickly has not been understood.",
    '1:7': "Fear of the LORD is called the beginning, not a later stage — and fools are marked by contempt, not ignorance.",
    '1:8': "The mother's teaching is called law, set beside the father's. Both voices carry authority in this house.",
    '1:9': "Parental instruction is described as jewellery rather than a leash — something worn, and visible on you.",
    '1:10': "Everything turns on the one word \"consent.\" Being enticed is not the sin; agreeing is.",
    '1:11': "\"For the innocent without cause.\" The gang has no grievance to settle. Appetite is the whole motive.",
    '1:12': "They borrow the grave's own language to describe themselves, and hear nothing wrong in it.",
    '1:13': "The recruiting pitch is prosperity. Sin rarely argues morality; it presents a business plan.",
    '1:14': "\"Let us all have one purse.\" What is really being offered is belonging, with the money as the pretext.",
    '1:15': "The counsel is not to out-argue them but to keep the foot off the road. Distance does what debate will not.",
    '1:16': "Their feet \"make haste.\" Evil here is not drifted into; it is hurried toward.",
    '1:17': "A bird will not fly into a net it has watched being spread. The comparison is not flattering to the men.",
    '1:18': "The ambush they set is described as laid for themselves. The trap does not know who laid it.",
    '1:19': "Greed \"taketh away the life of the owners thereof\" — the gain ends up owning the man who chased it.",
    '1:20': "Wisdom does her crying in the street. She is not hidden in a school, and nobody can plead they never heard.",
    '1:22': "\"Love simplicity.\" The naive is not neutral or merely uninformed; he is attached to being that way.",
    '1:23': "The spirit is poured out on those who turn at reproof. The gift follows the correction, not the merit.",
    '1:24': "The charge is refusal, not ignorance. A hand was stretched out and no one looked at it.",
    '1:26': "Wisdom laughs at the calamity. Scripture lets her say it, and does not soften it into sorrow.",
    '1:27': "Desolation and whirlwind: the ruin arrives as weather does, all at once and open to no negotiation.",
    '1:28': "They seek early and do not find. The text allows for a lateness that earnestness cannot undo.",
    '1:29': "\"Did not choose the fear of the LORD.\" Fear of God is presented here as something elected or declined.",
    '1:31': "The sentence is to be fed on their own way. Judgement here is getting exactly what was wanted.",
    '1:32': "What destroys the fool is his prosperity. The hazard named is success, not hardship.",
    '1:33': "Safety is described modestly — dwelling quietly, free from dread. Not triumph; the absence of a nagging fear.",
    # ---- Chapter 2 ----
    '2:1': "The chapter is one long sentence hanging on \"if.\" Nothing is promised until the conditions have been laid out.",
    '2:2': "Inclining the ear and applying the heart: attention described as posture before it is described as thought.",
    '2:3': "Crying and lifting up the voice. Wisdom is not absorbed by proximity; it is asked for out loud.",
    '2:4': "Seeking her as for hid treasure assumes she is buried. The digging is expected, and so is the payoff.",
    '2:5': "Every condition was about seeking wisdom, and what is found is the knowledge of God. The search ends elsewhere than it began.",
    '2:6': "After all that searching, \"the LORD giveth wisdom.\" The digging turns up what was being handed out.",
    '2:7': "\"Layeth up\" — the wisdom is stored in advance for the righteous, a reserve rather than a wage paid on completion.",
    '2:8': "God keeps the paths, not only the travellers. The road itself is under guard.",
    '2:9': "Understanding arrives as righteousness and equity. In this book insight that changes no dealings is not insight.",
    '2:10': "Wisdom has to be \"pleasant unto thy soul\" to do its work. Agreement is not enough; it must be enjoyed.",
    '2:11': "Discretion preserves from the inside. The protection is a formed judgement, not a fence built around the man.",
    '2:12': "The first thing the evil man is identified by is his talk. Froward speech precedes froward deeds.",
    '2:13': "They \"leave the paths of uprightness.\" The crooked once walked straight; this is desertion, not native darkness.",
    '2:14': "The worst word in the sentence is \"rejoice.\" Evil has stopped being done and started being enjoyed.",
    '2:16': "Her weapon is named as flattery. The danger is the same as the evil man's: words that go down easily.",
    '2:17': "She forsook \"the guide of her youth\" and forgot a covenant of God. The betrayal is vertical before it is domestic.",
    '2:18': "Her house \"inclineth unto death\" — the floor is tilted. Nothing has to be decided for the slide to continue.",
    '2:19': "\"None that go unto her return again.\" The proverb states it absolutely and offers no exception to lean on.",
    '2:20': "The point of all that deliverance is positive: to walk with good men. Rescue is for company, not for solitude.",
    '2:21': "Dwelling in the land is covenant language. Wisdom is tied to Israel's inheritance, not to private success.",
    '2:22': "\"Rooted out\" — the image is agricultural. The wicked are not evicted from the land; they are pulled out of it.",
    # ---- Chapter 3 ----
    '3:1': "The commandments are kept by the heart, not stored in the memory. Retention here is a matter of affection.",
    '3:2': "The promise attached to the teaching is length of days and peace — plainly this life, plainly this world.",
    '3:3': "Mercy and truth, the pair used of God's own covenant loyalty, are bound on outwardly and written in inwardly.",
    '3:4': "Favour with God and with man together. The verse refuses to treat the two audiences as rivals.",
    '3:5': "The command is not to stop thinking but to stop leaning. Your understanding may be used; it may not be rested on.",
    '3:6': "\"He shall direct thy paths.\" The guidance is an act of God's, not a set of directions handed over in advance.",
    '3:7': "The opposite of fearing the LORD turns out to be being wise in your own eyes. Self-assessment is the rival religion.",
    '3:8': "Health to the navel and marrow to the bones. Wisdom's effect is described bodily, not spiritually.",
    '3:9': "Firstfruits. What honours God is not the amount but the order — what comes off the top before anything is kept.",
    '3:10': "Barns and bursting presses: a rule of how the world generally runs, not a lever the giver gets to pull.",
    '3:11': "Two ways to mishandle correction are named — despising it, and simply growing tired of it.",
    '3:12': "Chastening is offered as evidence of sonship rather than of anger. Hebrews takes this verse up exactly so.",
    '3:13': "\"Findeth\" and \"getteth\" stand side by side: wisdom is both stumbled on and worked for, and the book won't choose.",
    '3:14': "Wisdom is priced against silver on the market's own terms. The comparison meets the merchant where he lives.",
    '3:15': "\"All the things thou canst desire\" — the comparison is made to exhaust the list rather than top it.",
    '3:16': "Long life is in her right hand and riches in her left. Even in a verse about wealth, the days come first.",
    '3:17': "Pleasantness and peace. The wisdom being urged is nowhere described as grim, and its rewards are not deferred.",
    '3:18': "A tree of life. The tree barred at Eden's gate is here held out to anyone who will lay hold of her.",
    '3:19': "The wisdom that orders a life is the wisdom that founded the earth. It is the grain of things, not a personal knack.",
    '3:21': "\"Let not them depart from thine eyes.\" Wisdom is held by continuing to look at it, not by having once learned it.",
    '3:22': "Before it is an ornament, it is \"life unto thy soul.\" The advice is spoken of as something that animates.",
    '3:24': "Sleep is offered as the proof. What a man trusts shows most clearly in how he lies down.",
    '3:25': "The fear to be unafraid of is named as sudden. Warning is exactly what this kind of trouble does not give.",
    '3:26': "The confidence is placed in the LORD himself, not in the wisdom the chapter has just spent ten verses praising.",
    '3:27': "The good is called owed. Withholding it is not tight-fistedness but keeping back what already belongs to another.",
    '3:28': "The sin named is the delay, and what convicts is the clause \"when thou hast it by thee.\"",
    '3:29': "\"Seeing he dwelleth securely by thee.\" It is his trust in you that makes the scheming a betrayal.",
    '3:30': "Striving \"without cause\" is forbidden, which quietly concedes that there is sometimes cause.",
    '3:31': "The warning against envying the oppressor assumes that violent men often do look enviable.",
    '3:32': "Set against abomination is not reward but intimacy: \"his secret is with the righteous.\"",
    '3:33': "Curse and blessing land on houses. Consequence in this book runs through households, not just individuals.",
    '3:34': "Grace to the lowly, scorn to the scorner. Both James and Peter reach for this line when describing God's basic bent.",
    '3:35': "Shame is called the fool's promotion. He is not demoted into it; he rises, and that is the joke.",
    # ---- Chapter 4 ----
    '4:1': "\"Ye children\" — the address widens from the one son. What a father says to one he says to the house.",
    '4:2': "He calls his own words doctrine and law. A parent's teaching is given the weight of torah, not of opinion.",
    '4:3': "Before he was a teacher he was somebody's tender and only beloved. The authority comes from having been taught.",
    '4:4': "David's charge to Solomon is quoted inside Solomon's charge to his son. Wisdom arrives third-hand and loses nothing.",
    '4:5': "\"Get wisdom\" — the verb is the merchant's. Wisdom is spoken of as something acquired, not as something dawning.",
    '4:6': "Forsaking and loving are the two options offered. Wisdom is treated as a person to be kept faith with.",
    '4:7': "\"With all thy getting get understanding.\" The price named is not a sum but everything else a man is acquiring.",
    '4:8': "You exalt her and she promotes you. The honour comes back, but only to the man who gave it first.",
    '4:9': "A crown is something she delivers, never something seized. Even wisdom's dignity arrives as a gift.",
    '4:11': "\"I have led thee in right paths.\" The teaching was not lecture but company along a road already walked.",
    '4:12': "The benefit shows up when running, not standing. Wisdom proves itself at speed, under pressure.",
    '4:13': "\"Take fast hold... let her not go.\" The grip has to be maintained, because the reason given is \"she is thy life.\"",
    '4:14': "The prohibition is about entering. Nothing has been done yet on that path, and that is the moment to refuse.",
    '4:15': "Avoid, pass not by, turn, pass away — four verbs for one act. The piling up is the argument.",
    '4:16': "They cannot sleep until harm is done. Mischief has stopped being chosen and become a nightly appetite.",
    '4:17': "Bread of wickedness and wine of violence: not what these men do but what they are living on.",
    '4:18': "The just path shines \"more and more.\" It is not described as bright at the start, only as brightening.",
    '4:19': "\"They know not at what they stumble.\" The worst of darkness is not the falling but never learning the cause.",
    '4:22': "The words are life \"to those that find them.\" The medicine is real, and it still has to be gone looking for.",
    '4:23': "Guard the spring, not the stream. Behaviour is treated as downstream of something that needs watching first.",
    '4:24': "A froward mouth is spoken of as a thing to be put away from you, as though speech were luggage a man carries.",
    '4:25': "The discipline is applied to the eyes. What a man permits himself to look at is treated as the decisive thing.",
    '4:26': "\"Ponder the path of thy feet.\" The call is to think about the route, not merely to be careful with the next step.",
    '4:27': "Neither to the right nor the left — Deuteronomy's phrase. Wisdom is not a balance between extremes but a road.",
    # ---- Chapter 5 ----
    '5:2': "The defence against seduction is placed in the son's own lips. What he says will decide what he does.",
    '5:3': "Honeycomb and oil. The warning opens by conceding the sweetness, because pretending otherwise persuades nobody.",
    '5:4': "Wormwood and a sword, and both are at \"her end.\" Nothing bitter is tasted on the way in.",
    '5:5': "Her feet are already going down. The man is not being tempted to stop somewhere; he is joining a descent.",
    '5:6': "\"Her ways are moveable, that thou canst not know them.\" There is no path here to ponder, and that is the danger.",
    '5:8': "Not nigh the door. The command is about where a man's feet go, which is cheaper than testing his willpower.",
    '5:9': "Honour and years, given to others. The cost is counted in reputation and time rather than in guilt.",
    '5:10': "The labour of a life ends up furnishing a stranger's house. Adultery is described as a transfer of property.",
    '5:11': "The mourning comes \"at the last,\" when the body is already spent. Regret arrives too late to be useful.",
    '5:12': "His lament is not about the woman. He traces it back to the moment he decided he did not need correction.",
    '5:13': "\"My teachers... them that instructed me.\" He had them. It was never a shortage of information.",
    '5:14': "\"In the midst of the congregation.\" The ruin happened while he was sitting among God's people every week.",
    '5:15': "Your own cistern. Faithfulness is pictured as drinking what is already yours, not as heroic self-denial.",
    '5:16': "Water scattered in the streets is water wasted. The image is of a precious thing poured where nobody owns it.",
    '5:17': "\"Only thine own.\" Exclusivity is named here as the blessing rather than as the restriction.",
    '5:18': "The alternative to adultery is not restraint but rejoicing. The remedy offered is a better pleasure.",
    '5:19': "\"Be thou ravished always with her love.\" Scripture is entirely unembarrassed about desire inside a marriage.",
    '5:20': "\"And why wilt thou?\" After all the warnings, the last appeal is to make the man explain himself.",
    '5:21': "The LORD ponders all his goings. Secrecy was the whole appeal of the thing, and it turns out not to exist.",
    '5:22': "The cords are his own sins. Nobody had to come and tie him; the habit did the binding.",
    '5:23': "He dies \"without instruction\" though he had teachers. To refuse instruction and to lack it come out the same.",
    # ---- Chapter 6 ----
    '6:1': "The danger is a handshake given for somebody else. The debt is real though the spending was not yours.",
    '6:2': "\"The words of thy mouth\" twice in one line. The snare was not sprung on him; he spoke it into existence.",
    '6:3': "\"Humble thyself.\" The escape costs pride, and the book counts that a cheap price for getting free.",
    '6:4': "No sleep until it is undone. A liability that most men would live with is treated as an emergency.",
    '6:5': "Roe and bird. He is told to see himself as prey, which is not how a man who signs a bond sees himself.",
    '6:6': "The sluggard is sent to be taught by an insect. The humiliation is part of the lesson.",
    '6:7': "No guide, overseer or ruler. What the ant lacks is exactly what the sluggard is waiting for.",
    '6:8': "Summer and harvest. She works to the season rather than to her mood, and that is the whole difference.",
    '6:9': "\"How long?\" The sluggard's fault is not an act but a duration, which is why nobody can name the day he fell.",
    '6:10': "A little, a little, a little. Ruin is spelled out in instalments small enough that none of them alarms anyone.",
    '6:11': "Poverty comes like a traveller and then like an armed man: on a schedule, and then taking what it wants.",
    '6:12': "He \"walketh with a froward mouth.\" The crooked speech is not an incident but his manner of moving through a room.",
    '6:13': "Eyes, feet, fingers. He says everything by signal, so there is nothing anyone can hold him to afterwards.",
    '6:14': "The signalling is traced back to the heart, and the product named is discord. He is manufacturing division.",
    '6:15': "\"Suddenly... suddenly... without remedy.\" The man who never spoke plainly gets a collapse with no warning either.",
    '6:16': "Six, yea seven. The old numerical formula exists to tell you the last item on the list is the one it was built for.",
    '6:17': "The list starts with a look. Pride is on the face before it ever reaches the tongue or the hands.",
    '6:18': "Heart and feet together — the thing devised inside and the speed with which the body carries it out.",
    '6:19': "Sowing discord among brethren is the seventh and the climax, set in company with murder and perjury.",
    '6:22': "Leads when walking, keeps when sleeping, talks when waking. The commandment is described as a companion, not a code.",
    '6:23': "Lamp and light. The law's work here is to show a man where he is standing, which is not the same as driving him.",
    '6:24': "All that light is aimed at one practical thing: surviving a flattering tongue.",
    '6:25': "\"In thine heart\" and \"with her eyelids.\" The transaction is complete before anybody has touched anybody.",
    '6:26': "A piece of bread against the precious life. Two prices are named, one absurdly small and one total.",
    '6:27': "The question assumes its answer. Every man who has done it believed he would be the exception.",
    '6:28': "The second question repeats the first, because men go on arguing with the first.",
    '6:29': "\"Shall not be innocent.\" That is a verdict, not a consequence. The guilt attaches at the touch.",
    '6:30': "The book concedes that a hungry thief is not despised. It is willing to weigh circumstances before it judges.",
    '6:31': "Even the pitied thief restores sevenfold. Theft is ruinous and it is payable; a price exists.",
    '6:32': "Against the thief who can repay, the adulterer is set as a man who \"destroyeth his own soul.\" No restitution is offered.",
    '6:33': "The wound heals. \"His reproach shall not be wiped away\" — what lasts is not the injury but the record of it.",
    '6:34': "Jealousy is called rage, not grievance. The husband is not described as a legal process to be managed.",
    '6:35': "No ransom, no gift. In a book that prices everything, this is the one door money does not open.",
    # ---- Chapter 7 ----
    '7:2': "\"As the apple of thine eye\" — guarded the way a man guards his eyesight, by reflex rather than by decision.",
    '7:3': "Bound on the fingers, where a working man keeps seeing them. The law is put where the hands can be watched.",
    '7:4': "Call wisdom sister. Against a seductress the counter offered is kinship — a woman a man cannot think of that way.",
    '7:6': "The father teaches by telling what he watched from a window. The warning comes as a case, not as a rule.",
    '7:7': "\"Among the simple ones.\" He was not singular. The young man in this story is the ordinary one in the crowd.",
    '7:8': "He takes the route that passes her corner. Nothing has happened yet and everything has been settled.",
    '7:9': "Twilight, evening, black night — the hour is given three times, which is how long he stayed out there.",
    '7:10': "The attire is obvious, the heart is subtil. What he can see about her is not the part that catches him.",
    '7:11': "Loud, stubborn, never at home. She is described by restlessness before she is described by immorality.",
    '7:12': "\"Lieth in wait at every corner.\" He thought he was wandering. He was being hunted.",
    '7:13': "The impudent face is part of the method. There is no shame to be got past, so nothing slows the thing down.",
    '7:14': "She opens with religion — offerings made, vows paid. Piety is the first tool she reaches for.",
    '7:15': "\"I have found thee.\" She makes him feel chosen, which is the flattery under all the other flattery.",
    '7:16': "Tapestry and fine linen of Egypt. The pitch is imported luxury, a world he could not otherwise enter.",
    '7:17': "Myrrh, aloes and cinnamon — the spices of worship and of burial, sprinkled on a bed.",
    '7:18': "\"Until the morning.\" The offer states its own expiry out loud, and he does not hear it.",
    '7:19': "The husband enters the story only as an absence, and only because she needs him for the argument.",
    '7:20': "\"The day appointed.\" She has consulted the calendar. Nothing about this evening was impulsive.",
    '7:21': "\"With the flattering of her lips she forced him.\" The verse calls persuasion force and does not soften it.",
    '7:22': "An ox to the slaughter goes quietly. What is missing from the description is any struggle at all.",
    '7:23': "\"Knoweth not that it is for his life.\" Ignorance kills him here, not defiance.",
    '7:25': "The heart declines first. By the time the feet go astray the decision is old news.",
    '7:26': "\"Many strong men have been slain by her.\" The casualty list is made of capable people, which is the warning.",
    '7:27': "Her house is called a way rather than a place. Nobody intends to arrive where it goes.",
    # ---- Chapter 8 ----
    '8:1': "\"Doth not wisdom cry?\" Set against the whisper on the dark corner, the question is unanswerable.",
    '8:2': "She stands where the paths meet. Wisdom takes up her position at the point where a choice is actually made.",
    '8:3': "The gates and the city entrance — the most public ground there is, and the exact opposite of the last chapter.",
    '8:4': "\"Unto you, O men.\" The address stops being a father's to a son and becomes an offer to everybody.",
    '8:5': "She calls fools to become understanding. The address assumes a man is not stuck with what he currently is.",
    '8:6': "She warrants her content before delivering it — excellent things, right things. Wisdom expects to be assessed.",
    '8:7': "Not only does she speak truth; wickedness is \"an abomination to my lips.\" She claims a distaste, not just a policy.",
    '8:8': "\"Nothing froward or perverse in them\" — the exact opposite of the man in chapter six who spoke with his fingers.",
    '8:9': "Plain \"to him that understandeth.\" The clarity depends on the hearer, which is an uncomfortable thing to be told.",
    '8:10': "Instruction and silver are set as alternatives, not as a ranking. The verse assumes you cannot chase both.",
    '8:12': "She keeps company with prudence and with witty inventions. Practical ingenuity is counted in, not looked down on.",
    '8:13': "The fear of the LORD is given content: it is hating evil. And the first evil listed is pride.",
    '8:14': "\"I have strength.\" Wisdom claims power here, not merely good advice to be taken or left.",
    '8:15': "\"By me kings reign.\" A ruler's legitimacy is tied to something he does not control, which is a claim on him.",
    '8:16': "The judges of the earth, not just of Israel. The claim is made over governments that never heard of her.",
    '8:17': "\"Those that seek me early shall find me\" — the same word that in chapter one came too late to help anybody.",
    '8:18': "\"Durable\" is the working word. And righteousness is listed among the riches rather than against them.",
    '8:19': "Fruit and revenue in one breath. She speaks the language of the field and of the counting-house at once.",
    '8:20': "She leads in the way of righteousness. The profit and the path are not two things to be balanced.",
    '8:21': "The treasures are filled for \"those that love me.\" Wisdom is inherited by affection, not extracted by technique.",
    '8:22': "\"The LORD possessed me in the beginning of his way.\" The church fought a century over whether that word means made.",
    '8:23': "\"From everlasting... or ever the earth was.\" Wisdom's credential is offered as sheer antiquity.",
    '8:24': "\"I was brought forth\" — the language is birth rather than manufacture, and the poem keeps choosing it.",
    '8:25': "Before the mountains were settled. The oldest, most permanent things a man can point at are junior to her.",
    '8:26': "\"The highest part of the dust of the world.\" After the heavens and the deeps, the poem stoops to name dirt.",
    '8:27': "\"When he set a compass upon the face of the depth\" — creation as draughtsmanship, and she watched it drawn.",
    '8:29': "The sea is given a decree it cannot pass. Even the least governable thing in the world is under a word.",
    '8:30': "\"Daily his delight, rejoicing always.\" The making of the world is described as play, not as labour.",
    '8:31': "After the depths and the heavens, her delights land on the sons of men. The cosmic poem ends up at people.",
    '8:32': "After all that height, the ask is small: keep her ways. Nothing exotic is required of the people addressed.",
    '8:33': "\"Refuse it not.\" The one thing forbidden is stated as a refusal, which is the only move a hearer really has.",
    '8:34': "Watching daily at the gates, waiting at the posts. The posture is a servant's, or a suitor's — outside the door.",
    '8:35': "Life is what is found, and favour is what is obtained. Neither verb is one a man does to earn something.",
    '8:36': "\"All they that hate me love death.\" Indifference to wisdom is not neutrality; it is named as an affair.",
    # ---- Chapter 9 ----
    '9:1': "Wisdom builds and hews before she invites. Her house exists; Folly, later in the chapter, only sits in a doorway.",
    '9:2': "Beasts killed, wine mingled, table furnished. Everything was ready before a single guest was asked.",
    '9:3': "Maidens sent out and a cry from the highest places. The invitation is delegated and it is loud.",
    '9:4': "The invitation is addressed to the simple. The least qualified people in the city are the ones named.",
    '9:5': "\"Come, eat of my bread.\" Wisdom is not transmitted as information here but taken as a meal.",
    '9:6': "\"Forsake the foolish, and live.\" What has to be left is company, which is harder than leaving habits.",
    '9:7': "The man who corrects a scorner gets hurt for it. The book says so plainly instead of commending the attempt.",
    '9:8': "How a man takes a rebuke is offered as the whole diagnostic. Nothing else in the chapter sorts people so fast.",
    '9:9': "Instruction multiplies what is already there. Teaching is described as compounding, which explains the gap.",
    '9:10': "The book's opening line returns with wisdom in place of knowledge, and adds \"the knowledge of the holy.\"",
    '9:11': "Wisdom herself makes the promise of long life. It is not a reward arranged by a third party.",
    '9:12': "\"Thou alone shalt bear it.\" Neither the profit nor the loss can be transferred to anyone else.",
    '9:13': "Clamorous, simple, knowing nothing. Folly is loud in exact proportion to how little she has to say.",
    '9:14': "She sits at her door in the same high places. Folly counterfeits wisdom's address without having built anything.",
    '9:15': "She calls to people \"who go right on their ways.\" Her targets are chosen from among those already headed somewhere.",
    '9:16': "Her invitation is word for word wisdom's. Listening to the wording will not tell anyone which door he is at.",
    '9:17': "She does not deny that the waters are stolen. She sells the theft itself as the flavour.",
    '9:18': "The dead are already in the room. Nobody mentioned them, and the guest is not told who else accepted.",
    # ---- Chapter 10 ----
    '10:1': "Gladness to a father, heaviness to a mother. The same son is felt differently in the two halves of the house.",
    '10:2': "Not that the treasures are not real — only that they \"profit nothing\" at the one point where profit is wanted.",
    '10:3': "The promise is that the righteous will not famish. It is a floor under a man, not a ceiling raised over him.",
    '10:4': "Poverty is traced to a hand. This book's usual case is not fate or injustice but what a man did all week.",
    '10:5': "The fault is timing. He is not called lazy for sleeping but for sleeping in harvest, the one month it costs.",
    '10:6': "Blessings worn on the head, violence stopping the mouth. Each man ends up wearing what he has dealt in.",
    '10:7': "Reputation keeps moving after death — one name blessed, the other rotting. Neither is finished when the man is.",
    '10:8': "Receiving commandments is set against prating. The fool's problem is not that he disagrees but that he is talking.",
    '10:9': "\"He that perverteth his ways shall be known.\" The crooked man's exposure is treated as a matter of time.",
    '10:10': "The wink does its harm without a word anyone can quote back at him.",
    '10:11': "\"A well of life\" — a righteous man's speech is described as a public utility that others come and draw from.",
    '10:12': "\"Love covereth all sins\" — Peter quotes it. Covering here is not hiding the wrong but declining to publish it.",
    '10:13': "The rod is for a back, not a mind. What cannot be got in through words is not therefore left untaught.",
    '10:14': "The wise lay knowledge up against a day. The fool's mouth spends the whole store the moment he has it.",
    '10:15': "Stated flatly and without moral comment: money really does protect. Proverbs is honest about that before it is critical.",
    '10:16': "The wicked man's fruit \"tendeth to sin.\" His gain does not sit still; it compounds into more of what ruined him.",
    '10:17': "The man who keeps instruction is himself \"in the way\" — he becomes a road that other people can walk on.",
    '10:18': "Hidden hatred and open slander are both called folly. Keeping it in does not make the quieter man the better one.",
    '10:19': "Simple arithmetic: more words, more sin. Restraint is offered as a technique, not as a temperament.",
    '10:20': "The tongue is priced as silver and the heart as worthless. Speech is being valued by what stands behind it.",
    '10:21': "The righteous feed many while fools die of what they lack. One mouth is a supply, the other a hunger.",
    '10:22': "The weight is on the second clause. What marks God's giving is not the size of it but the absence of the usual price.",
    '10:23': "\"As sport.\" The fool is not driven into mischief by need or pressure. He is entertained by it.",
    '10:24': "Each man is granted his inner life — the wicked his dread, the righteous his desire. Both get what they carried.",
    '10:25': "The whirlwind does not sort anybody. What differs between the two men is only what they are founded on.",
    '10:26': "Vinegar and smoke. The sluggard is described from the employer's side, as an irritation rather than a tragedy.",
    '10:27': "Fear of the LORD is given a plain measurable effect, and the shortening of the wicked is the same sum reversed.",
    '10:28': "The wicked had expectations too. That is the sting of the verse — not that he hoped for nothing.",
    '10:29': "One and the same \"way of the LORD\" is strength to the upright and destruction to the rest. The road does not change.",
    '10:31': "\"The froward tongue shall be cut out.\" The judgement is aimed at the organ that did the work.",
    '10:32': "Knowing \"what is acceptable\" is a sense of fit, not only of right. The righteous man is being credited with tact.",
    # ---- Chapter 11 ----
    '11:1': "A merchant's scale is made the object of God's delight or disgust. Worship and weights in the same sentence.",
    '11:2': "\"When pride cometh, then cometh shame.\" No interval is allowed between them; they travel as one arrival.",
    '11:3': "Integrity is said to guide. Honesty is treated as an instrument for finding the way, not merely as a fence.",
    '11:4': "Riches are not called worthless, only useless on one particular day. The verse names the day.",
    '11:5': "\"The wicked shall fall by his own wickedness.\" No executioner is needed; the thing is self-administering.",
    '11:6': "\"Taken in their own naughtiness\" — the language of a trap, sprung on the men who built it.",
    '11:7': "What dies with him is his expectation. The thing he was living toward turns out to have been perishable.",
    '11:8': "\"The wicked cometh in his stead.\" A substitution is stated and never explained, and the book leaves it there.",
    '11:9': "The hypocrite works by mouth and the just is delivered by knowledge. Against talk, the defence is knowing things.",
    '11:10': "A whole city's mood is said to rise and fall with who is prospering in it. Righteousness is treated as public weather.",
    '11:11': "A city is exalted by blessing and overthrown \"by the mouth\" — a town can be lost through what is said in it.",
    '11:12': "Set against despising a neighbour is not praising him but holding your peace. Silence is what love does here.",
    '11:13': "Faithfulness is defined by what a man does not repeat. It is measured in the conversations that never happen.",
    '11:14': "The book that praises the wise man also distrusts one man's judgement, and says so without embarrassment.",
    '11:15': "\"He that hateth suretiship is sure.\" A settled aversion is recommended in place of judging each case on merit.",
    '11:16': "Honour is retained by grace and riches by strength. Two different currencies, and the verse declines to rank them.",
    '11:17': "Mercy and cruelty are both described as landing first on the man himself, before they reach anyone else.",
    '11:18': "\"A deceitful work.\" The wicked man's wages are not merely lost; they lie to him while he is earning them.",
    '11:19': "He pursues evil and what he overtakes is his own death. The chase is real; the quarry is not what he thought.",
    '11:20': "Abomination and delight are set on hearts, not on deeds. God is described as reacting to a shape, not a tally.",
    '11:21': "\"Though hand join in hand.\" Numbers are specifically ruled out as a defence; conspiracy changes nothing.",
    '11:22': "The gold is still gold. Beauty is not denied in this insult — it is placed where it cannot do anything but glint.",
    '11:23': "The righteous desires and the wicked expects, and wrath is what arrives. It is the expecting that makes it bitter.",
    '11:24': "Scattering increases and hoarding impoverishes. And \"more than is meet\" concedes that some withholding is right.",
    '11:25': "\"He that watereth shall be watered.\" The return comes back in the same currency it went out in.",
    '11:26': "Withholding corn earns a public curse. The grain market is treated as a moral arena, not a private calculation.",
    '11:27': "The seeking is what procures. A man looking for good finds favour as a by-product of the looking.",
    '11:28': "Trusting riches against flourishing as a branch. A branch is green precisely because it holds nothing up itself.",
    '11:29': "\"Inherit the wind.\" A man who wrecks his own household ends up owning something he cannot put his hands on.",
    '11:30': "\"He that winneth souls is wise.\" Wisdom is finally measured by its effect on other people, not by its possession.",
    '11:31': "The argument runs from the righteous being recompensed here on earth, not around it. Peter uses the verse that way.",
    # ---- Chapter 12 ----
    '12:1': "Hating reproof is called brutish — the word for an animal. Refusing correction is placed below reasoning, not against it.",
    '12:2': "Condemnation falls on \"wicked devices\" — on the plans, which is earlier than most men expect to be judged.",
    '12:3': "The wicked is established and the righteous is rooted. One is built up from outside, the other holds from below.",
    '12:4': "A crown is worn where everyone sees it; rottenness works in the bones. The two harms run on different timescales.",
    '12:5': "The comparison is made at the level of thoughts. Righteousness is traced back behind the words to the counsel.",
    '12:6': "Words that \"lie in wait for blood\" and a mouth that delivers. Both murder and rescue are done here by talking.",
    '12:7': "\"Overthrown, and are not.\" The wicked do not decline gradually; what is contrasted is a house that stays standing.",
    '12:8': "Commendation eventually tracks wisdom. Reputation is not reliable quickly, but the verse says it settles honestly.",
    '12:9': "Better despised with a servant than honoured with no bread. Keeping up appearances is priced here in food.",
    '12:10': "How a man treats his animals is made a moral index. And the wicked are cruel at the exact point they mean to be kind.",
    '12:11': "The alternative to tilling your land is not rest but company — \"vain persons.\" Idleness comes with people attached.",
    '12:12': "He desires the net. What the wicked envies is not the plunder but the apparatus that other men use to take it.",
    '12:13': "The just \"come out of trouble\" rather than avoid it. Deliverance here is an exit, not an exemption.",
    '12:14': "The fruit of the mouth is set beside the work of the hands, as though talk were a trade a man is paid for.",
    '12:15': "\"Right in his own eyes.\" The fool is not defined by being wrong but by being satisfied with his own verdict.",
    '12:16': "The fault named is speed — his wrath is \"presently known.\" The anger itself is not what separates him from the prudent.",
    '12:17': "Telling the truth is called shewing forth righteousness. Testimony is treated as an act of justice, not of candour.",
    '12:18': "Piercings of a sword against a tongue that heals. Both are precise instruments; the difference is what they are for.",
    '12:19': "The trouble with a lie is its duration. The verse concedes it works, and dates it at a moment.",
    '12:20': "Joy is attached to the counsellors of peace. Peacemaking is paid, and the payment is described as internal.",
    '12:21': "\"There shall no evil happen to the just.\" It is stated bare, with no qualification, and the book lets it stand.",
    '12:22': "Truth is something a man deals in, like goods. It is an action here before it is a property of a sentence.",
    '12:23': "The prudent conceals what he knows. Knowing when not to display knowledge is counted as part of having it.",
    '12:24': "Diligence ends in ruling and slackness in tribute. Ordinary work is what quietly decides who gives the orders.",
    '12:25': "The cure for a stooping heart is a good word — somebody else's sentence, which the man cannot supply himself.",
    '12:26': "\"The way of the wicked seduceth them.\" They are described as taken in by their own road rather than as choosing it.",
    '12:27': "He does not even cook what he caught. Laziness is shown wasting the one thing that came to the man for free.",
    '12:28': "\"In the pathway thereof there is no death.\" The boldest line in the chapter, and the only one that outruns this life.",
    # ---- Chapter 13 ----
    '13:1': "The wise son hears instruction; the scorner does not even hear rebuke. Raising the volume reaches him no better.",
    '13:2': "What a man says is a crop he has to eat. The transgressor's own harvest is described as violence.",
    '13:3': "Keeping the mouth and keeping the life are the same verb. Guarding one is stated as guarding the other.",
    '13:4': "The sluggard desires. Appetite is not the difference between him and the diligent man, and the verse says so first.",
    '13:5': "The righteous hates lying, and the wicked becomes loathsome. One is a choice, the other is what a man ends up smelling of.",
    '13:6': "Righteousness keeps a man \"in the way\" — it guards someone already walking rather than fetching him from elsewhere.",
    '13:7': "Both frauds get named: the man performing wealth and the man performing poverty. Neither is left as the honest one.",
    '13:8': "Riches ransom a life, but nobody bothers threatening a man with nothing. Poverty's one advantage, stated coldly.",
    '13:9': "One light rejoices and one lamp is put out. Neither man's light is his own doing; only one of them keeps burning.",
    '13:10': "\"Only by pride.\" One cause is named for every quarrel there is, which leaves nothing to blame on the other party.",
    '13:11': "Wealth is sorted by how it arrived, not by how much of it there is. Vanity shrinks and labour compounds.",
    '13:12': "Hope deferred is described as an illness. The book grants that waiting does real damage rather than building character.",
    '13:13': "\"He that feareth the commandment.\" Fear here is directed at a word, which the verse treats as an attainable posture.",
    '13:14': "A fountain keeps giving. And its stated purpose is evasive: to get a man away from snares he would otherwise walk into.",
    '13:15': "\"The way of transgressors is hard.\" Not punished — hard. The road itself is rough, before anyone judges anything.",
    '13:16': "The fool \"layeth open\" his folly. He is not caught out; display is his method, and he thinks it is candour.",
    '13:17': "A messenger's faithfulness is health to the man who sent him. Whoever delegates is exposed to another man's character.",
    '13:18': "Refusing instruction costs poverty and shame together. The price is charged to the purse as well as to the face.",
    '13:19': "The halves do not match, and that is the point: fools will not hand back what they want, even for the sweetness offered.",
    '13:20': "Wisdom passes by proximity. A man is shaped by his company without anybody in it intending to teach him.",
    '13:21': "\"Evil pursueth sinners.\" It hunts them. They are not standing still waiting for consequences to catch up.",
    '13:22': "The sinner's wealth is \"laid up for the just.\" He has been working as a custodian and was never told.",
    '13:23': "The poor man's field produces plenty. What destroys is want of judgment — a failure in the court, not in the furrow.",
    '13:24': "The rod and love are identified with each other, and \"betimes\" makes promptness part of the loving.",
    '13:25': "The difference is not the size of the meal but where a man stops. One eats to satisfying; the other's belly still wants.",
    # ---- Chapter 14 ----
    '14:1': "She plucks it down \"with her hands.\" The house is not lost through neglect; the demolition takes effort.",
    '14:2': "Walking uprightly is simply called fearing the LORD. The inward thing is read straight off the outward one.",
    '14:3': "\"A rod of pride\" in his own mouth. What beats the fool is the stick he has been carrying between his teeth.",
    '14:4': "A clean stable means an empty one. Mess is named as the cost of anything that actually produces.",
    '14:5': "The second half is a tautology on purpose: character decides testimony, which is why an oath settles nothing.",
    '14:6': "The scorner does seek wisdom and does not find it. Seeking is not enough when the posture is wrong.",
    '14:7': "The counsel is to leave. No argument is offered, no correction attempted — just get out of the room.",
    '14:8': "The prudent man's wisdom is to understand \"his way\" — his own. Set against it is deceit, which starts with the self.",
    '14:9': "Fools mock at sin. Comedy is the defence, because a joke about a thing need never be answered.",
    '14:10': "\"A stranger doth not intermeddle with his joy.\" The loneliness of the inner life is stated, and no remedy follows.",
    '14:11': "The wicked have a house and the upright a tent, and it is the permanent structure that comes down.",
    '14:12': "\"Seemeth right.\" The danger is not a road that looks wrong but one that passes every test a man knows to run.",
    '14:13': "Laughter with sorrow inside it. The book allows that cheerfulness is sometimes the surface of something else.",
    '14:14': "Both men are filled from themselves. The backslider is not punished with something foreign; he gets his own contents.",
    '14:15': "\"The simple believeth every word.\" Credulity, not ignorance, is what the book keeps meaning by simplicity.",
    '14:16': "The wise man is the one who is afraid, and the fool is the confident one. The usual valuation is reversed.",
    '14:17': "Quick temper is called foolish and scheming is called hated. One makes a man ridiculous, the other makes him despised.",
    '14:18': "Folly is inherited rather than committed here — received like an estate nobody chose to be left.",
    '14:19': "The bowing happens \"at the gates of the righteous.\" The reversal is placed on the good man's own doorstep.",
    '14:20': "Hated even by his own neighbour. The verse reports how poverty is actually treated before anyone says it is wrong.",
    '14:21': "The correction to the previous line arrives immediately: despising a neighbour is not sociology but sin.",
    '14:22': "Both parties devise. The difference is not that one plans and the other drifts, but what the planning is for.",
    '14:23': "\"In all labour there is profit.\" The thing set against work is not rest but talk about work.",
    '14:24': "The wise man's riches are called a crown; the fool has nothing to show but more of what he already was.",
    '14:25': "\"A true witness delivereth souls.\" Lives turn on what is said in court, which makes testimony a rescue operation.",
    '14:26': "The fear belongs to one man and the refuge extends to his children. Something is being sheltered forward a generation.",
    '14:27': "The phrase used of the wise man's teaching is here given to the fear of the LORD. The fountain has moved upstream.",
    '14:28': "A king's honour is counted in people. Greatness is measured by how many are there, not by what was conquered.",
    '14:29': "Slowness to anger is called great understanding. Patience is filed under intelligence rather than under temperament.",
    '14:30': "Envy is described as a disease working in the bones — not a feeling about someone else but damage to the envier.",
    '14:31': "The insult passes through the poor man and lands on his Maker, who is also the oppressor's. Both were made.",
    '14:32': "\"The righteous hath hope in his death.\" Hope is located at the one point where the book's usual promises run out.",
    '14:33': "Wisdom rests quietly in the wise; in fools it \"is made known.\" You can hear which man has less of it.",
    '14:34': "The book steps up from the household to the nation, and keeps the same measure for both.",
    '14:35': "A king's favour follows usefulness. Royal service is described without any sentiment about loyalty.",
    # ---- Chapter 15 ----
    '15:1': "The soft answer works on somebody else's wrath. What is being claimed is leverage over another man, not calm in yourself.",
    '15:2': "\"Useth knowledge aright.\" Knowledge is a tool here, and the fool is not short of it — he simply pours it out.",
    '15:3': "\"Beholding the evil and the good.\" The same watching covers both, which makes it attention before it is surveillance.",
    '15:4': "A tree of life against a breach in the spirit. Words are credited with reaching something nobody can see or repair.",
    '15:6': "Treasure in the righteous house, trouble in the wicked man's revenues. The trouble is inside the income, not after it.",
    '15:8': "The wicked man brings an animal and the upright brings words. The cheaper offering is the one called delight.",
    '15:9': "He loveth \"him that followeth after righteousness\" — the man still pursuing it, not the man who has caught up.",
    '15:10': "Correction is grievous to the one who has already left the way. The further out he is, the more the rescue hurts.",
    '15:11': "The argument runs from the grave to the heart: if the dead lie open to God, a living man's inside is the easier case.",
    '15:12': "He will not go unto the wise. The scorner's defence is not argument but staying away from anyone who might correct him.",
    '15:13': "The face reports the heart, and then the traffic reverses: sorrow does not merely show, it breaks the spirit.",
    '15:14': "Both men are eating. One seeks knowledge and the other feeds on folly; the appetite is identical, the diet is not.",
    '15:15': "\"All the days of the afflicted are evil.\" The book grants that first, flatly, before it offers the other case.",
    '15:16': "\"And trouble therewith.\" The trouble is not sent as a punishment for the treasure; it arrives in the same package.",
    '15:17': "The stalled ox is the better food and the worse dinner. What is weighed is not the meal but who is sitting at it.",
    '15:18': "The same man stirs strife or settles it, and only his speed decides which. Quarrels are shown to have a supplier.",
    '15:19': "A hedge of thorns across his own road. The sluggard's obstacles are reported from inside his head, where they are real.",
    '15:20': "The son has become \"a foolish man\" and the grief has become contempt. The verse follows the household forward in time.",
    '15:22': "Purposes are \"disappointed\" for want of counsel. They fail at the planning stage, before anybody has done anything wrong.",
    '15:23': "\"A word spoken in due season, how good is it!\" The joy is the speaker's, and what pleases him is the timing.",
    '15:24': "The way of life is upward. Wisdom's reward is given as a direction of travel rather than as a place arrived at.",
    '15:25': "A proud man's house and a widow's boundary stone. God works in property lines, pulling one down and holding the other.",
    '15:27': "Greed \"troubleth his own house.\" The man takes for his family, and the damage lands on precisely them.",
    '15:28': "The righteous heart studies before it answers. Delay is treated as a moral act rather than as slowness of wit.",
    '15:29': "Far from the wicked. The distance is God's and is stated as position, not as deafness or as unwillingness.",
    '15:30': "Good news is said to reach the bones. The body is where this book keeps locating what we would call morale.",
    '15:31': "The ear that hears reproof \"abideth among the wise.\" Membership follows from listening, and nothing else is asked.",
    '15:32': "Refusing instruction is called despising your own soul. The injury is self-inflicted and aimed at the one thing that matters.",
    '15:33': "Humility stands before honour as a sequence, not as a tactic. Attempted the other way round it stops working.",
    # ---- Chapter 16 ----
    '16:1': "Both the planning and the saying are handed to the LORD. What is left to the man is not named, and the verse does not say.",
    '16:2': "\"Clean in his own eyes.\" The remedy offered is not a more honest self-examination but a set of scales he does not hold.",
    '16:3': "Commit the works and the thoughts are established. The order is odd: the doing goes over first, and the mind settles after.",
    '16:4': "\"Even the wicked for the day of evil.\" The worst thing is put inside the plan rather than outside it, and not softened.",
    '16:5': "Pride is called an abomination while it is still in the heart. No act is required before the charge is laid.",
    '16:6': "Mercy and truth purge iniquity; the fear of the LORD only keeps men away from it. Two separate jobs, and pardon is the first.",
    '16:7': "Even his enemies. The peace is made by God with the man's opponents, over his head and without his negotiating anything.",
    '16:8': "The little is not praised for being little. What makes it better is the one word standing next to it, righteousness.",
    '16:9': "The man does devise. His planning is granted outright and then quietly overruled at the level of each step he takes.",
    '16:10': "The king's mouth \"transgresseth not in judgment\" — said as a description of the office, which makes it the standard too.",
    '16:11': "The weights in the merchant's bag are called God's work. The tools of the trade belong to someone before they belong to him.",
    '16:12': "Wickedness is an abomination to kings, not only to God. The throne rests on the thing a king could most easily abuse.",
    '16:14': "Messengers of death, and a wise man pacifies them. Managing a powerful man's temper is filed here as a skill.",
    '16:15': "The king's face as the latter rain. Favour from a throne is treated as weather: needed, welcome, and nobody's to command.",
    '16:16': "\"How much better.\" No argument is offered for it; the comparison with gold is simply asserted, and by a man who had both.",
    '16:17': "The highway of the upright is departing from evil. The road is named by what it leads away from, not by where it arrives.",
    '16:18': "Two orderings and no agent. Nobody is said to push; pride is described as arriving first and the fall as coming behind it.",
    '16:19': "What humility costs here is not loss but spoil. There is a share being declined, not merely a poverty being endured.",
    '16:20': "Handling a matter wisely and trusting the LORD sit in one sentence, and happiness is attached to the second of them.",
    '16:21': "Sweetness of the lips \"increaseth learning.\" How a thing is said is credited with how much of it actually gets through.",
    '16:22': "A wellspring \"unto him that hath it.\" Understanding is shown feeding the man who holds it before it reaches anybody else.",
    '16:24': "Honeycomb: sweet to the soul and health to the bones. Kind words are counted here as food rather than as decoration.",
    '16:25': "Word for word what was already said at 14:12. The repetition is the only comment the collection makes on it.",
    '16:26': "Hunger is named as the engine. The book grounds honest labour in appetite and offers no apology for doing so.",
    '16:27': "He \"diggeth up\" evil — it took work to find — and the lips that carry it away are already alight.",
    '16:28': "A whisperer separates chief friends. The closest friendships are named as what the quietest method takes apart.",
    '16:29': "He enticeth his neighbour. The violent man's first act is persuasion, and it is done to somebody who lives nearby.",
    '16:30': "Shut eyes and moving lips. The verse reads a man's face and finds the thing already under way behind it.",
    '16:31': "\"If it be found in the way of righteousness.\" The crown is conditional, and age on its own is given no honour at all.",
    '16:32': "A taken city is set beside a man's own spirit, and the smaller territory is called the harder conquest.",
    '16:33': "The lot is genuinely cast. Chance is not denied but assigned; the randomness is exactly where the disposing happens.",
    # ---- Chapter 17 ----
    '17:1': "The full house is the religious one — \"sacrifices\" means feast meat. Quiet beats even holy abundance.",
    '17:2': "Competence crosses the blood line. The inheritance follows wisdom where birth has failed to produce it.",
    '17:3': "Every metal has an assayer. The heart's is the LORD, which means no one else is qualified to read it.",
    '17:4': "Slander needs a hearer to survive. The listener is named in the same breath as the liar, not excused.",
    '17:5': "Mockery of the poor is redirected upward to the one who made him. Poverty is not read as a verdict on a man.",
    '17:6': "Honour runs both directions down the generations. The old are crowned by the young and the young by the old.",
    '17:7': "The argument climbs from the absurd to the intolerable: a fine phrase misfits a fool, a lie destroys a ruler.",
    '17:8': "\"In the eyes of him that hath it\" — this is the briber's own estimate, reported rather than endorsed.",
    '17:9': "Covering is set against repeating, not against confronting. It is the retelling that separates friends.",
    '17:10': "A hundred stripes change nothing the man is unwilling to hear. Correction works on receptiveness, not on force.",
    '17:11': "The rebel's punishment arrives in the shape of his own choice: someone harder than himself, sent.",
    '17:12': "The bear is the safer meeting because a bear is predictable. Folly is what you cannot plan around.",
    '17:13': "Evil returned for good does not visit the man and leave. It takes up residence in the house.",
    '17:14': "Strife is a breach in a dam. The only moment anyone controls is the first one, before the water is moving.",
    '17:15': "Both verdicts are called abomination — which is what makes God's justifying of the ungodly cost a cross.",
    '17:16': "Wisdom can be paid for and still not obtained. What the fool lacks is appetite, not access.",
    '17:17': "Not two kinds of relationship but one test. Adversity is what discloses which of them you actually had.",
    '17:18': "The folly happens \"in the presence of his friend.\" Pledges are signed under social pressure, not after arithmetic.",
    '17:19': "The quarreller and the man who builds a grand doorway are one character. Both are making a display.",
    '17:20': "The crooked heart needs no separate punishment. Finding no good is simply what crookedness does.",
    '17:21': "Stated without remedy. The father of a fool is given no advice here, only the sorrow.",
    '17:22': "Cheerfulness and despair are treated as physical facts — medicine and dried bones — not as moods.",
    '17:23': "\"Out of the bosom.\" The bribe passes where it cannot be seen, and the concealment is the confession.",
    '17:24': "The fool's trouble is distance. He looks to the ends of the earth for what is standing in front of him.",
    '17:25': "The mother is added this time. One foolish son is counted twice over in the same house.",
    '17:26': "Punishing the innocent is named as a crime of the bench, not of the street.",
    '17:27': "Restraint is offered as evidence of knowledge. The man with the most to say is not the one saying it.",
    '17:28': "Half a joke, and it holds: silence buys a reputation the fool has not earned and cannot keep talking.",
    # ---- Chapter 18 ----
    '18:1': "The separating is done for desire's sake. What isolation produces is quarrel dressed as independent thought.",
    '18:2': "He is not listening. He is waiting for his turn, and understanding would only delay it.",
    '18:3': "Contempt arrives as the wicked man's travelling companion rather than as his eventual sentence.",
    '18:4': "Deep waters and a flowing brook are not the same thing. Depth is worth little until it is made available.",
    '18:5': "Favouritism is never neutral. Accepting one man's face is spelt out as overthrowing another's case.",
    '18:6': "The fool's mouth calls for the strokes. He is not ambushed by the beating; he sends for it.",
    '18:7': "\"The snare of his soul\" — a trap he set himself, then walked into without recognising his own work.",
    '18:8': "Gossip is described as swallowed, not resisted. It goes down easily because it is received as food.",
    '18:9': "Doing nothing is put in the same family as smashing things. Neglect is destruction at a slower speed.",
    '18:10': "The tower is a name. It is entered by running into it, which is the one thing a builder cannot do.",
    '18:11': "Set deliberately beside the verse before. The only difference between the two towers is \"in his own conceit.\"",
    '18:12': "The order cannot be reversed. Humility is the road to honour, never the fee charged afterwards.",
    '18:13': "Folly and shame both — the first is what it is, the second is what it looks like to everyone watching.",
    '18:14': "The spirit carries the rest of a man. The question is what is left underneath when the spirit itself is cut.",
    '18:15': "Getting and seeking in one verse. Knowledge arrives at the ear that had already gone out looking.",
    '18:16': "How influence actually moves is described flatly, with no verdict attached. Proverbs can report without approving.",
    '18:17': "One side always sounds just. What makes it sound just is only the absence of the other.",
    '18:18': "A decision no one can take credit for is what ends a fight between men too strong to concede.",
    '18:19': "Nearness builds the wall. The strong city here is made of how much the offended brother once trusted you.",
    '18:20': "A man eats what he says. Speech is treated as a crop he will have to live on afterwards.",
    '18:21': "\"They that love it shall eat the fruit thereof.\" The lover of talk gets whichever of the two he has grown.",
    '18:22': "A marriage is counted here as favour received from the LORD, not as a prize the man's qualities secured.",
    '18:23': "Poverty pleads and wealth answers roughly. The verse describes the effect of money on manners and defends neither.",
    '18:24': "The closest tie in the verse is not the one you were born into. Friendship can outrun blood.",
    # ---- Chapter 19 ----
    '19:1': "The comparison is poverty against folly, not poverty against wealth. Integrity is what is being weighed.",
    '19:2': "Haste is called sin, not merely error. Moving faster than you understand is treated as a moral act.",
    '19:3': "A man wrecks his own road and then frets at God about the wreckage. The blame moves faster than the memory.",
    '19:4': "What money buys is company. The verse says friends, and then describes what happens when the money stops.",
    '19:5': "The court has already been deceived, which is why the punishment of a false witness is left to God.",
    '19:6': "Everyone is a friend to the man handing out gifts, and that sentence tells you what the friendship is for.",
    '19:7': "The cruel detail is the last line: he keeps pursuing them with words after they have already gone.",
    '19:8': "Wisdom is framed as loving your own soul. Getting understanding is self-preservation, not self-denial.",
    '19:10': "Delight for a fool, rule for a servant — the verse is about things landing where nothing earned them.",
    '19:11': "Letting an offence pass is called a man's glory, a word usually spent on how well he avenged it.",
    '19:12': "Lion and dew from the same man. Neither his rage nor his favour is something you can negotiate for.",
    '19:13': "Not violence but a leak. The contentious house wears a man down by repetition rather than by force.",
    '19:14': "The line separates what fathers can hand on from what they cannot. A prudent wife is not in the estate.",
    '19:15': "Idleness is not a pause. Slothfulness deepens itself into sleep, and the hunger arrives while he is under.",
    '19:16': "Keeping the commandment is described as keeping your own soul. The law is presented as a fence, not a fee.",
    '19:17': "God makes himself the debtor for whatever is given to a man who cannot possibly repay it.",
    '19:18': "\"While there is hope\" — the verse assumes a window, and assumes it closes.",
    '19:19': "Rescue him from the consequence and you have removed the only teacher he was listening to.",
    '19:20': "Counsel is taken young and collected old. Wisdom is described as something you will be glad of later.",
    '19:21': "Not that planning is wrong — that only one of all these plans is under no obligation to fail.",
    '19:22': "What is wanted from a man is kindness. A poor man with nothing to give has at least not promised it falsely.",
    '19:23': "The fear of the LORD produces satisfaction, which is a stranger result than the success one might expect.",
    '19:24': "Comic exaggeration, and exact: the sluggard starves with his hand already in the dish.",
    '19:25': "Some men learn only by watching what lands on someone else. The scorner is struck for the simple man's benefit.",
    '19:26': "The crime is domestic and the reproach is public. What is done indoors does not stay there.",
    '19:27': "The command is to stop listening. Some instruction is not to be answered or corrected, only left alone.",
    '19:28': "Iniquity is eaten with appetite. The wicked mouth is not tempted into it; it is hungry for it.",
    '19:29': "\"Prepared.\" The judgements are not improvised when the scorner appears. They were already made.",
    # ---- Chapter 20 ----
    '20:1': "The drink is given the agency and the man is called deceived. Nobody sets out to be mocked by wine.",
    '20:2': "The harm named is to himself. Provoking a king is sin against his own soul, not against the throne.",
    '20:3': "Walking away is counted as honour — in a culture where honour is normally what keeps a man in the fight.",
    '20:4': "The sluggard has a reason, and the cold is real. It is simply not a reason that produces a harvest.",
    '20:5': "The skill praised is drawing another man out. Understanding is here a kind of listening, not a stock of answers.",
    '20:6': "Goodness is common as a claim and rare as a fact. The verse counts the proclamations and then asks for one man.",
    '20:7': "What the children inherit is the walking. Integrity is passed down more reliably than an estate.",
    '20:8': "The throne's work is done by looking. Being seen clearly is itself the judgement that scatters.",
    '20:9': "The question expects no hand to go up — which is precisely why cleansing has to arrive from outside the man.",
    '20:10': "Fraud too small for anyone to notice is named with the word otherwise reserved for idolatry.",
    '20:11': "Character is legible early, and the child never consented to be read.",
    '20:12': "The faculties you use to judge the world were made by the one who will be judging you.",
    '20:13': "The warning is against loving sleep, not against sleeping. The appetite is the problem, not the rest.",
    '20:14': "\"It is naught, it is naught\" — the haggler's lie is socially permitted, and the boast afterwards names it as a lie.",
    '20:15': "Gold and rubies are merely listed. The rare thing in the verse is a man who says something worth hearing.",
    '20:16': "The advice is to the lender. A man who guarantees strangers has already shown what kind of risk he is.",
    '20:17': "The sweetness is conceded without argument. The whole case is made in the word \"afterwards.\"",
    '20:18': "War is the example chosen. The decision least open to consultation is the one most in need of it.",
    '20:19': "Gossip and flattery are traced to the same man. Whoever discusses others with you is discussing you elsewhere.",
    '20:20': "The lamp that goes out is the family's own. Contempt for the source extinguishes what came from it.",
    '20:21': "What arrives too quickly is not blessed at the end. The verse says nothing against the inheritance itself.",
    '20:22': "The alternative to revenge is not resignation. It is waiting, on the understanding that someone else will act.",
    '20:24': "Providence is given as the reason self-knowledge runs out. A man cannot map a road he is not steering.",
    '20:25': "The enquiry comes after the vow. The order is the snare — devotion spoken first and costed later.",
    '20:26': "Threshing language. The wheel is not for punishment alone but for separating what was mixed together.",
    '20:27': "Conscience is called the LORD's candle in a man, which makes it his light and not the man's own.",
    '20:28': "The throne is held up by the softest thing in the verse. Mercy is named twice, power not at all.",
    '20:29': "Each age is given an honour of its own, and neither can borrow the other's without looking ridiculous.",
    '20:30': "Pain is treated as a washing. The proverb states it without apology and offers no gentler version.",
    # ---- Chapter 21 ----
    '21:1': "The freest man in the kingdom is the one being turned, and he is turned like water, not forced like stone.",
    '21:2': "Self-assessment is not the court. A man grades his own way and the LORD weighs the heart it came out of.",
    '21:3': "Worship can be offered instead of obedience. The verse names the exchange and refuses it.",
    '21:4': "Even the plowing is included. Pride does not confine itself to the proud moments of a wicked man's life.",
    '21:5': "Diligence is set against haste, not against idleness. Hurry and laziness arrive at the same want.",
    '21:6': "The treasure is real and the getting is fatal. The liar succeeds at exactly the thing that is killing him.",
    '21:7': "The robbery itself does the destroying. No separate punishment is needed once the method is in place.",
    '21:8': "The crooked way is also called strange — unfamiliar even to the man who has been walking it for years.",
    '21:9': "The wide house is the prize and the trap in one sentence. A roof corner is preferred to it.",
    '21:10': "Appetite, not circumstance. The wicked soul wants evil, so the neighbour never had a chance of favour.",
    '21:11': "Two routes to the same knowledge, and only one of them costs a beating. The wise take the cheaper.",
    '21:12': "Watching a house come down is treated as instruction rather than entertainment.",
    '21:13': "The deafness is measured back to him in kind. He will find out what his own silence sounded like.",
    '21:14': "The mechanics of a bribe are described with no verdict attached. Secrecy is what makes it work.",
    '21:15': "The same act gladdens one man and destroys another. Justice is not neutral ground.",
    '21:16': "The wandering is aimless and the destination is not. He drifts, and arrives somewhere exact.",
    '21:17': "It is the loving, not the having. Wine and oil are not forbidden; being in love with them is the ruin.",
    '21:18': "Stated as how the world settles accounts — and precisely reversed at the cross, where the righteous ransoms the wicked.",
    '21:20': "Wisdom shows in what is still there. The fool is not described as earning less, only as having nothing left.",
    '21:21': "What is pursued turns out to be what is found. Righteousness is both the road and the thing waiting on it.",
    '21:22': "Wisdom takes the walls that strength built. The mighty city falls to the man who never could have built it.",
    '21:23': "Guarding the mouth is described as guarding the soul. Most trouble is let in through the same door.",
    '21:24': "The man is not described but named. Scorner is what he is called now, and the name has stopped being an insult.",
    '21:25': "He is not a man without desire. Desire is the thing killing him, because his hands will not go and get it.",
    '21:26': "Both are all-day habits. Coveting and giving are set side by side as two ways of spending a life.",
    '21:27': "The sacrifice was already abomination before the motive was mentioned. The motive only makes it worse.",
    '21:28': "The man who listened before he spoke can keep speaking. The one who invented his evidence runs out.",
    '21:29': "One man manages his face, the other manages his direction. Only one of those can be kept up.",
    '21:30': "Not that wisdom fails against God, but that there is no such category. Counsel against him does not exist.",
    '21:31': "Preparation is assumed and not credited. The horse is still saddled; it is simply not what saves.",
    # ---- Chapter 22 ----
    '22:1': "A name is the one asset a man cannot buy back, which is why it is chosen \"rather than\" and not alongside.",
    '22:2': "What the rich and poor have in common is not their meeting but their Maker, and that is the leveller.",
    '22:3': "The simple are not called wicked. They are punished for walking on, which is the least dramatic of sins.",
    '22:4': "Humility and the fear of the LORD are put together as one thing, and the reward is described, not promised as a trade.",
    '22:5': "The thorns are in the way itself. The froward man does not meet trouble; he is travelling through it.",
    '22:6': "\"The way he should go\" is a direction, not a trade. What is trained early is what he will return to late.",
    '22:7': "Debt is described as a form of ownership. The verse states it flatly, without advising either party.",
    '22:8': "The rod of his anger fails. The instrument the oppressor relied on breaks before his victims do.",
    '22:9': "The blessing attaches to the eye before the hand. Generosity is a way of seeing, and the bread follows.",
    '22:10': "The contention leaves with the man. Remove him and the argument turns out to have had no other source.",
    '22:11': "Pure heart and gracious speech together. The king's friendship comes as a by-product, not as the aim.",
    '22:12': "God preserves knowledge itself, not only knowers. Truth is being kept alive whether or not anyone keeps it.",
    '22:13': "\"There is a lion without.\" The sluggard's excuse is dramatic because a dull one would not be believed.",
    '22:14': "The pit is a mouth. What catches the man is talk, and he falls in by listening.",
    '22:15': "Foolishness is bound in, not picked up. The child is not corrupted by the world; he arrives with it.",
    '22:16': "Two ways to end poor: robbing the poor and flattering the rich. Both are investments in the wrong direction.",
    '22:17': "A new collection opens by asking for the ear before the mind. Hearing is the posture, knowing comes after.",
    '22:18': "Kept within, then fitted in the lips. Words come out well only after they have been stored a long time.",
    '22:19': "The stated purpose of the whole collection is trust in the LORD, not competence in life.",
    '22:21': "The point of certainty is being able to answer whoever sent you. Assurance is for reporting back.",
    '22:22': "\"Because he is poor\" — the poverty is named as the reason he is robbed. It is what makes him safe to rob.",
    '22:23': "The LORD takes the case himself. The poor man's disadvantage in the gate is answered by a different court.",
    '22:24': "The warning is not to reform the angry man but to avoid him. Some traits are contagious.",
    '22:25': "Character is caught, not chosen. The snare is that you will have his temper before you notice acquiring it.",
    '22:26': "Sureties are grouped with striking hands — the gesture, the ceremony, the moment of feeling generous.",
    '22:27': "The bed under you. The verse names the specific thing you will lose rather than the general risk.",
    '22:28': "A landmark is a stone somebody else set. Honouring it means accepting limits you did not choose.",
    '22:29': "Diligence in ordinary business is what puts a man before kings. The promotion runs through the work itself.",
    # ---- Chapter 23 ----
    '23:1': "Eating with power is described as a situation requiring attention. The danger is at the table, not outside it.",
    '23:2': "A knife to the throat is the figure chosen for self-control. The instruction is violent because the appetite is.",
    '23:3': "\"Deceitful meat.\" The food is real; what is false is what accepting it is understood to mean.",
    '23:4': "\"Cease from thine own wisdom\" — the labour to be rich is treated as a cleverness, not merely an effort.",
    '23:5': "Riches make themselves wings. Nobody takes them; they leave under their own power, and upward.",
    '23:6': "The grudging host is more dangerous than an open enemy, because the meal itself is the pretence.",
    '23:7': "\"His heart is not with thee\" while his mouth says eat. The hospitality and the man are two different things.",
    '23:8': "You will lose the sweet words too. The flattery spent to get the meal is wasted along with the meal.",
    '23:9': "The advice is to stop talking. Not every hearing is worth the wisdom it would cost you.",
    '23:10': "The fatherless are named because their field is the one with no one to defend the boundary.",
    '23:11': "\"Their redeemer is mighty.\" The kinsman missing from the orphan's family turns out to be God.",
    '23:12': "Heart and ears together. Instruction is not received by attention alone, nor by willingness alone.",
    '23:13': "The verse answers the fear, not the duty: the parent who flinches is told the child will not die.",
    '23:14': "The stakes are named as the soul, which is why the discipline is not discussed as a matter of temperament.",
    '23:15': "\"Even mine\" — the teacher admits the whole labour has been for his own gladness as well as the son's good.",
    '23:17': "Envy of sinners is answered with the fear of the LORD \"all the day long,\" not with a better view of sinners.",
    '23:18': "\"Surely there is an end.\" The argument against envy is that the present is not the final reckoning.",
    '23:19': "The heart is guided, not merely informed. Wisdom is steering, and the son does it himself.",
    '23:20': "The company is named before the vice. You become a drunkard by sitting with them first.",
    '23:21': "Drowsiness clothes a man with rags. The poverty is described as arriving quietly, by way of sleep.",
    '23:22': "\"When she is old\" — the temptation named is not rebellion but the slow contempt of a parent who has weakened.",
    '23:23': "Buy it and never sell. Truth costs something to get and there is no market where parting with it pays.",
    '23:24': "The father's joy is put forward as a motive, which is a smaller and more human argument than most here.",
    '23:26': "\"Give me thine heart.\" After all the instruction, what is finally asked for is not compliance.",
    '23:27': "A ditch and a narrow pit — both are described by how hard they are to climb out of, not by how they look.",
    '23:28': "\"She lieth in wait as for a prey.\" The encounter that felt like chance was arranged.",
    '23:29': "Six questions with no answer yet. The symptoms are listed first so the reader diagnoses before he is told.",
    '23:30': "The answer to all six is one habit, described not as drunkenness but as lingering.",
    '23:31': "The warning is against looking. By the time the cup is in hand the argument has already been lost.",
    '23:32': "\"At the last.\" The serpent is not in the first glass, and everything depends on that delay.",
    '23:33': "The damage is to perception itself. A man cannot notice what drink is doing with the faculties drink has altered.",
    '23:34': "A mast in a heaving sea. The image is not of pleasure but of having nothing underneath you that holds still.",
    '23:35': "\"I will seek it yet again.\" The verse ends inside the man's own voice, still planning, having felt none of it.",
    # ---- Chapter 24 ----
    '24:1': "Envy of evil men is assumed to be a real temptation, and the reason given is their company, not their gains.",
    '24:2': "Their conversation gives them away. What the heart studies, the lips eventually talk about.",
    '24:3': "A house is built by wisdom before it is built by hands. The order is deliberate.",
    '24:4': "Knowledge fills the chambers. The riches in the house arrive through understanding rather than alongside it.",
    '24:5': "Strength is redefined as something that increases with knowing rather than declining with age.",
    '24:6': "War is the test case again. Even the decision that looks like it needs one commander needs many counsellors.",
    '24:7': "Wisdom is \"too high\" for the fool, so he keeps silent in the gate — the one place where speech settles matters.",
    '24:8': "The name follows the scheming. Reputation is not unfair here; it is accurate reporting.",
    '24:9': "The thought of foolishness is already sin. Nothing has to be acted on for the account to be opened.",
    '24:10': "Adversity does not weaken strength; it measures it. The day of trouble is a reading, not a cause.",
    '24:11': "The sin named is forbearing — not doing the harm but declining to interrupt it.",
    '24:12': "\"We knew it not\" is answered by the one who weighs hearts. Ignorance is a claim, and it can be checked.",
    '24:13': "Honey is commended without apology. Wisdom's argument begins with something simply good to taste.",
    '24:14': "The comparison holds all the way through: wisdom is sweet, and the sweetness is not the reason to take it.",
    '24:15': "The wicked man is addressed directly, mid-collection, as if he were in the room listening.",
    '24:16': "The just man is not the one who does not fall. He is the one who is up again, seven times over.",
    '24:17': "Gladness at an enemy's fall is forbidden while the fall itself is not. The verse polices the heart only.",
    '24:18': "The reason given is startling: your gloating could turn God's anger away from him. Judgement is not yours to enjoy.",
    '24:19': "Fretting and envying are named together, which is a fair account of how the two actually feel.",
    '24:20': "The candle put out. The argument against envying the wicked is entirely about how briefly it lasts.",
    '24:21': "Fear of God and fear of the king in one clause, and the warning is against the appetite for upheaval.",
    '24:22': "\"Who knoweth the ruin of them both?\" The revolutionary cannot calculate what he is starting.",
    '24:23': "Partiality in judgement heads a new collection, as though it were the first thing the wise must not do.",
    '24:24': "Calling the wicked righteous earns the curse of nations. The false acquittal is treated as a public injury.",
    '24:25': "Rebuke is called a delight, which is not how it is usually experienced by either party.",
    '24:26': "A straight answer is received like a kiss. Honesty is described as an intimacy, not as a bluntness.",
    '24:27': "Field first, house afterwards. The order is the whole counsel: build what feeds you before what shelters you.",
    '24:28': "\"Without cause\" — the verse leaves room for testifying, and closes it against testifying for sport.",
    '24:29': "Repayment in kind is quoted in the first person, because that is the voice it always arrives in.",
    '24:30': "The lesson begins with a walk past a field. Wisdom here is got by noticing, not by revelation.",
    '24:31': "Thorns, nettles, a broken wall. Nothing was done to the vineyard; that is exactly what was done to it.",
    '24:32': "He looked, considered, and received instruction. The three steps are separated because most people stop at the first.",
    '24:33': "\"A little\" three times over. Ruin is priced in instalments small enough that no single one looks like a decision.",
    '24:34': "Poverty comes as a traveller and want as an armed man. It arrives on schedule and then it takes.",
    # ---- Chapter 25 ----
    '25:1': "Hezekiah's reform included copying clerks. Part of what a revival did was preserve old words nobody had lost yet.",
    '25:2': "Hiddenness is named as God's glory, not as a defect in him. The creature's honour is the searching.",
    '25:3': "The king's heart is filed with the height of heaven and the depth of earth — among the things you do not get to know.",
    '25:4': "Nothing is added to the silver. The vessel appears once what was never silver has been taken out.",
    '25:5': "The throne is established by subtraction. Righteousness here is the result of removals, not of a new policy.",
    '25:6': "Advancing yourself in the king's presence is treated as a technical error before it is a moral one.",
    '25:7': "\"Come up hither.\" Jesus tells this proverb back as a parable, and it keeps its edge about the seating.",
    '25:8': "The risk is not losing the quarrel but reaching the end of it with nothing left to say.",
    '25:9': "Keep the argument between the two of you. A third party turns a dispute into evidence.",
    '25:10': "\"Thine infamy turn not away.\" The quarrel will be settled long before the reputation is.",
    '25:11': "\"Fitly\" carries the whole verse. The same words at the wrong moment are not gold and not a gift.",
    '25:12': "The ornament needs both parts. Correction becomes gold only where there is an ear willing to wear it.",
    '25:13': "Snow in harvest refreshes the masters, not the messenger. Reliability is felt by the people who had to trust it.",
    '25:14': "Clouds and wind and no rain. The boast raised expectations, and raised expectations are the injury.",
    '25:15': "A soft tongue breaks bone. The gentlest instrument in the verse is credited with the hardest work.",
    '25:16': "A good thing is limited by your capacity, not by its goodness. Honey is not the problem; sufficiency is.",
    '25:17': "Welcome is a finite resource. The neighbour's affection can be spent, and the spending feels like friendship.",
    '25:18': "Maul, sword, arrow — three weapons at three ranges. False testimony can reach a man from anywhere.",
    '25:19': "A broken tooth and a dislocated foot fail exactly when used. An unreliable man is not useless; he is worse.",
    '25:20': "Cheerfulness offered to grief is put alongside stealing a coat in winter. The intention is not the question.",
    '25:21': "Bread and water for the enemy — the plainest possible provisions, nothing symbolic about the kindness.",
    '25:22': "Coals of fire and a reward. Paul takes this up whole, and neither he nor Solomon explains the coals.",
    '25:23': "Backbiting needs a face that welcomes it. A cold look does what argument cannot.",
    '25:25': "Distance is what makes the news precious. You could not go and see, so you had to wait and not know.",
    '25:26': "A fountain everyone drinks from. When a righteous man gives way, the damage is public water, not private failure.",
    '25:27': "Honey and self-praise share a defect: both are pleasant until the quantity makes them the point.",
    '25:28': "Self-control is described as fortification. Without it a man is not merely undisciplined; he is undefended.",
    # ---- Chapter 26 ----
    '26:1': "Snow in summer is not harmful, only wrong. Honour on a fool is an offence against season, not against him.",
    '26:2': "A curse without cause has nowhere to land. Words are not treated here as having power of their own.",
    '26:3': "Whip, bridle, rod. The fool is grouped with animals not to insult him but to say what he responds to.",
    '26:4': "The risk of answering: you argue on his terms and end up indistinguishable from him.",
    '26:5': "The risk of not answering, set immediately beside the opposite instruction. The contradiction is the counsel.",
    '26:6': "Sending word by a fool is described as cutting off your own feet. You chose him; the damage is yours.",
    '26:7': "A proverb in a fool's mouth hangs there like a lame leg. The truth is intact and cannot carry anything.",
    '26:8': "Honour given to a fool comes back at you like a stone tied into a sling. It does not go where it was aimed.",
    '26:9': "The drunkard cannot feel the thorn he is waving. A fool quoting wisdom is dangerous and does not know it.",
    '26:10': "The verse is hard, and what survives every reading of it is that God is the one handing out the wages.",
    '26:11': "The image is chosen to disgust. Returning to folly is not weakness here; it is appetite for what was already expelled.",
    '26:12': "The ranking is the point: a fool can still be taught, and a man certain of his own wisdom cannot.",
    '26:13': "The excuse has grown a second lion since last time. Fear invented to order always expands.",
    '26:14': "A door on its hinges moves constantly and goes nowhere. The sluggard is not still; he is just not travelling.",
    '26:15': "\"It grieveth him.\" The effort of feeding himself is experienced as a hardship, and that is the whole joke.",
    '26:16': "Laziness arrives with certainty attached. Seven men with reasons cannot outvote a sluggard's opinion of himself.",
    '26:17': "A dog by the ears: you cannot hold on and you cannot let go. Other people's quarrels have no safe exit.",
    '26:18': "The madman is introduced first, with firebrands and arrows, before we learn who he is a picture of.",
    '26:19': "\"Am not I in sport?\" The defence is the offence. Calling it a joke is how the damage is meant to be waived.",
    '26:20': "Strife is treated as a fire needing fuel. Remove the supply and nothing has to be extinguished.",
    '26:21': "Some men are not caught in the argument. They are the wood, and they were carried in for the purpose.",
    '26:23': "Silver dross over a potsherd — the coating is itself waste. Warm words on hatred are a cheap plating.",
    '26:24': "The lips dissemble while the deceit is laid up inside. Hatred here is stored, not spent.",
    '26:25': "\"Believe him not\" is the instruction, against the evidence of pleasant speech. Discernment overrules courtesy.",
    '26:26': "Concealment has a term limit. The congregation is where it ends, which means publicly and among his own people.",
    '26:27': "The pit and the stone both come back. Neither requires an avenger; the mechanism is the man's own labour.",
    '26:28': "The liar hates the person he has damaged. Injury creates the hatred rather than following from it.",
    # ---- Chapter 27 ----
    '27:1': "Not boasting of tomorrow is grounded on ignorance, not on modesty. You do not know what the day holds.",
    '27:2': "Praise is not forbidden, only self-issued praise. The same words from a stranger are worth having.",
    '27:3': "Stone and sand can be set down. A fool's anger is weighed against them because it cannot.",
    '27:4': "Wrath and anger are cruel and survivable. Envy is the one with no exhaustion point, because nothing satisfies it.",
    '27:5': "Love that never says anything is ranked below rebuke. Silence is not the safer kindness.",
    '27:6': "The wounds are called faithful. Which of the two is hurting you is not how you tell friend from enemy.",
    '27:7': "Hunger, not the food, decides the taste. Appetite is what makes bitter things sweet and honey tiresome.",
    '27:8': "A bird off the nest is not free; it is exposed. Wandering from your place is described as loss of cover.",
    '27:9': "Counsel from a friend is put with perfume — something that works on the heart before it works on the problem.",
    '27:10': "Proximity outranks blood in a crisis. The friendship your father kept up is worth more than a distant brother.",
    '27:11': "The father wants his son's wisdom as an answer to those who reproach him. Teaching has a stake in its results.",
    '27:14': "Loud blessing at dawn is counted a curse. Praise with bad timing and volume attached is received as an attack.",
    '27:15': "\"In a very rainy day.\" The leak is bad enough; the point is that outside offers no escape either.",
    '27:16': "Restraining her is like holding wind or gripping oil. The verse concedes the attempt is hopeless.",
    '27:17': "Iron on iron makes sparks and noise. Sharpening is not described as a comfortable process between friends.",
    '27:18': "Service and fruit-growing are the same arrangement. Waiting on a master is treated as tending something that yields.",
    '27:19': "You learn your own heart by looking at another man's. The mirror in the verse is somebody else.",
    '27:20': "The eye is put in the same sentence as the grave. Neither fills, and both go on receiving.",
    '27:21': "Praise is the furnace. What a man does with being admired assays him more exactly than hardship does.",
    '27:22': "Ground with a pestle among wheat, and the folly stays. Not all failure responds to pressure.",
    '27:23': "Know the state of your flocks. Attention to the actual animals is the counsel, not strategy about them.",
    '27:24': "The crown is put next to the riches and asked the same question. Nothing inherited is exempt from running out.",
    '27:25': "Hay, grass, herbs. The reason to work is that the ground keeps offering, and the offer is seasonal.",
    '27:26': "Lambs clothe you and goats buy the field. The flock is described as the means, never as the treasure.",
    '27:27': "Enough — for the household and the maidens too. Sufficiency, carefully counted, is the end of the passage.",
    # ---- Chapter 28 ----
    '28:1': "Nobody is chasing him. Guilt supplies its own pursuer, and courage turns out to be a matter of conscience.",
    '28:2': "Many princes are a symptom. Instability at the top is read downward as a verdict on the land.",
    '28:3': "A poor man oppressing the poor is a rain that destroys the crop. The one who knows the hunger does the worst damage.",
    '28:4': "Praise of the wicked is treated as the visible form of abandoning the law. Approval is a position.",
    '28:5': "Evil men do not understand judgement — not that they reject it. Moral failure is described as an inability to see.",
    '28:6': "\"Though he be rich\" is conceded rather than denied. The comparison is made with the advantage left in place.",
    '28:7': "The son's company is what shames the father. He is judged by who he eats with before anything he has done.",
    '28:8': "The usurer is gathering for somebody else. He is a collector on behalf of the man who will pity the poor.",
    '28:9': "A closed ear makes the prayer abomination. Refusing to hear God is treated as a reason he need not hear you.",
    '28:10': "The trap is sprung by its own maker. Leading the righteous astray is how a man finds the pit he dug.",
    '28:11': "The poor man sees through him. Wealth buys the appearance of wisdom from everyone except those with nothing to gain.",
    '28:12': "\"A man is hidden.\" Bad government is measured by how many people have made themselves hard to find.",
    '28:13': "Covering and confessing are the alternatives, and mercy is attached to the one that gives up the cover.",
    '28:14': "Fear is called happiness here. The hardened heart is the opposite of the fearing one, not the braver version of it.",
    '28:15': "Lion and bear. The wicked ruler is described as wildlife, which means as something with no case to answer.",
    '28:16': "Oppression is traced to a lack of understanding rather than to malice. The stupid prince is the dangerous one.",
    '28:17': "\"Let no man stay him.\" The proverb refuses to intervene for the murderer, which is an unusual silence.",
    '28:18': "\"Shall fall at once.\" The perverse man does not decline gradually; the whole crooked road ends in one step.",
    '28:19': "Tilling against following. The alternative to work is not rest but attaching yourself to empty people.",
    '28:20': "The faithful man abounds and the hasty one is not called poor but guilty. Speed costs innocence first.",
    '28:21': "\"For a piece of bread.\" The price at which a judgement can be bought is set humiliatingly low.",
    '28:22': "The man in a hurry for money has an evil eye and no arithmetic. He does not consider, which is the whole failure.",
    '28:23': "Favour arrives afterwards. Rebuke is a delayed investment and flattery pays out immediately, which is why it wins.",
    '28:24': "\"It is no transgression.\" The theft is bad; the redefinition of it as nothing is what makes him a destroyer's companion.",
    '28:25': "Pride is identified as the engine of strife, and trust in the LORD as what makes a man easy to live with.",
    '28:26': "Trusting your own heart is named as folly outright, which rules out sincerity as a guide.",
    '28:27': "\"Hideth his eyes.\" Not refusing the poor but declining to look at them, which is how it is usually done.",
    # ---- Chapter 29 ----
    '29:1': "\"Often reproved\" — the warnings were themselves the mercy, and running out of them is what \"without remedy\" means.",
    '29:2': "A people's mood is treated as evidence. Rejoicing and mourning are how a nation files its report on the men in charge.",
    '29:3': "Loving wisdom and keeping harlots are both ways of spending what a father handed over. Only one of them gladdens him.",
    '29:4': "Judgment establishes the land and gifts overthrow it. Bribery is described as demolition rather than as a favour.",
    '29:5': "The net is being spread for his feet while the flattery is still in his ears. He enjoys the trap as it is laid.",
    '29:6': "The evil man's own transgression is the snare. The righteous is not merely spared; he is the one left singing.",
    '29:7': "\"Regardeth not to know it.\" The wicked do not refuse the poor man's case. They arrange never to hear it.",
    '29:8': "Scorn is treated as a civic hazard. Mockery can snare a whole city, and wise men spend themselves turning the wrath away.",
    '29:9': "\"Whether he rage or laugh, there is no rest.\" Both of the fool's moods close the conversation the same way.",
    '29:10': "Integrity itself is what draws the hatred. The upright man gave no offence except being what he is.",
    '29:11': "\"Till afterwards.\" The wise man is not silent, only delayed, and the delay is the entire difference.",
    '29:12': "A ruler who listens to lies ends up staffed by liars. A court is shaped by whatever the throne rewards.",
    '29:13': "Poor man and oppressor meet, and the LORD lights the eyes of both. The shared gift settles nothing between them.",
    '29:14': "The throne is secured by how the poorest are judged. Permanence is promised for the cases nobody was watching.",
    '29:15': "Rod and reproof together. A child left to himself is not neutral ground, and the shame arrives on his mother.",
    '29:16': "\"The righteous shall see their fall.\" The proverb does not deny the increase of wickedness; it outlasts it.",
    '29:17': "Correction is promised to give rest and delight. The discipline is described by what it does to the one giving it.",
    '29:18': "\"Vision\" is prophetic revelation, not ambition. Without a word from God a people is unrestrained, and restraint is the mercy.",
    '29:19': "\"Though he understand he will not answer.\" The failure is not comprehension. The words arrived and moved nothing.",
    '29:20': "Hasty words rank a man below the fool. There is hope for slow stupidity and less for a quick tongue.",
    '29:21': "Indulgence from childhood ends with the servant as heir. Kindness with no boundary quietly redraws the household.",
    '29:22': "The angry man supplies the strife and the furious man supplies the quantity. Temper is measured here by volume of sin.",
    '29:23': "Pride does the bringing low by itself, with no outside agent. Honour arrives at the humble from somewhere else entirely.",
    '29:24': "\"Heareth cursing, and bewrayeth it not.\" He keeps quiet under the oath, and the soul he hates turns out to be his own.",
    '29:25': "Fear of man is called a snare, which means it works on ground a man was already walking across.",
    '29:26': "Many crowd the ruler's door while the verdict is being handed down elsewhere. Access is not the same thing as judgment.",
    '29:27': "The abomination runs both ways. The chapter closes conceding that the upright are as intolerable to the wicked as the reverse.",
    # ---- Chapter 30 ----
    '30:1': "Agur comes with no tribe and no story attached, and his words are kept as prophecy regardless.",
    '30:2': "The oracle opens with a man calling himself brutish. Everything he goes on to know starts from that admission.",
    '30:3': "\"The knowledge of the holy.\" He claims neither schooling nor acquaintance with God, and then asks the questions anyway.",
    '30:4': "Every question has one answer, and the last of them — \"what is his son's name\" — is left standing open.",
    '30:5': "Purity of the word and shield for the one who trusts are put in a single sentence. Doctrine is described as cover.",
    '30:6': "Adding to God's words is classed as lying. The additions do not have to be false to make the man one.",
    '30:7': "The one prayer in Proverbs, and it is filed against the day of his death rather than for any present need.",
    '30:8': "\"Food convenient for me.\" He asks to be kept from both extremes because he knows which way each of them would take him.",
    '30:9': "Plenty is expected to produce denial and want to produce theft. Both ends of the scale arrive at the name of God.",
    '30:10': "Accusing another man's servant is warned against on grounds of risk rather than justice. The curse lands and stays.",
    '30:11': "The generation is identified by what it withholds — cursing the father, and merely not blessing the mother.",
    '30:12': "Pure in their own eyes and unwashed. Self-assessment is the one instrument that cannot pick this up.",
    '30:13': "Nothing is alleged about what they do. The whole indictment is carried by how the eyelids are held.",
    '30:14': "Teeth as swords, set to devour the poor. Predation is described as anatomy rather than as policy.",
    '30:15': "\"Give, give.\" The leech's daughters have no vocabulary past the demand, and the list that follows is built on them.",
    '30:16': "Grave, barren womb, dry earth, fire. Two of the four are griefs and two are appetites, and none can be brought to an end.",
    '30:17': "The mocking eye is the organ singled out for the ravens. The penalty is aimed at the particular contempt.",
    '30:18': "\"Which I know not.\" He assembles the four not to explain them but to record the point where explanation ran out.",
    '30:19': "Air, rock and sea leave no trace of what crossed them, and a man's way with a maid is listed as the fourth.",
    '30:20': "\"She eateth, and wipeth her mouth.\" The wonder of the previous verse turns sinister: another way that leaves no mark.",
    '30:22': "A servant reigning and a fool with his belly full. The earth cannot bear men handed more than they can carry.",
    '30:23': "The unloved wife married and the maid displacing her mistress. The disquiet comes from reversals inside a house.",
    '30:24': "Little and exceeding wise. The four are chosen precisely because size and wisdom turn out to be unrelated.",
    '30:25': "The ants are not strong and they prepare in summer. Foresight is offered as the substitute for power.",
    '30:26': "The conies are feeble and they build in rock. Weakness answered by choosing the ground rather than the fight.",
    '30:27': "Locusts have no king and still go forth by bands. Order on display with nobody issuing it.",
    '30:28': "The spider takes hold with her hands and ends up in palaces. Persistence gets in where rank cannot.",
    '30:30': "The lion turns not away for any. The stateliness is located in what he declines to do.",
    '30:31': "The first creature's name is lost to us; what survives the verse is a king walking as though nothing opposed him.",
    '30:32': "\"Lay thine hand upon thy mouth.\" The remedy for having spoken foolishly is issued as a physical instruction.",
    '30:33': "Churning, wringing, forcing — one verb three times. Press anything hard enough and it yields what it was always going to.",
    # ---- Chapter 31 ----
    '31:1': "The words are Lemuel's and the teaching is his mother's. A king's instruction is preserved under her authorship.",
    '31:2': "\"The son of my vows.\" She reminds him that he was prayed for before she tells him anything at all.",
    '31:3': "Kings are warned off women before anything else. What destroys a throne is domestic before it is political.",
    '31:4': "\"It is not for kings\" — said twice inside one verse. The prohibition attaches to the office, not to the drink.",
    '31:5': "The reason is given plainly: he will forget the law, and the afflicted are the ones who pay. Sobriety is owed to somebody else.",
    '31:6': "The strong drink refused to the king is prescribed for the dying. The bottle was never the moral question.",
    '31:7': "Forgetting is offered as a mercy to the man with nothing and denied as a luxury to the man with everything.",
    '31:8': "\"Open thy mouth for the dumb.\" Royal speech is assigned to the people who have none of their own.",
    '31:9': "The command comes again with content attached. Pleading the cause of the poor is the job itself, not a virtue added to it.",
    '31:10': "\"Who can find\" opens the portrait as a question. She is called rare before a single thing about her is described.",
    '31:11': "He shall have no need of spoil. Her husband is not enriched by plunder, because what he has at home already works.",
    '31:12': "\"All the days of her life.\" The good is not an episode. What is being praised is a duration.",
    '31:13': "Wool and flax, and \"worketh willingly.\" The willingness is recorded as carefully as the raw material.",
    '31:14': "Merchants' ships — she goes further for the food than she has to. There is an import trade behind the family table.",
    '31:15': "Up while it is still night, and the maidens get a portion too. Authority in this house begins by feeding it.",
    '31:16': "\"She considereth a field, and buyeth it.\" She weighs it first, and the purchase is hers to make.",
    '31:17': "Girding the loins and strengthening the arms. The vocabulary is a soldier's, spent here on domestic work.",
    '31:18': "\"She perceiveth that her merchandise is good.\" The appraisal is her own, and the candle burning late follows from it.",
    '31:19': "Spindle and distaff, the plainest tools in the portrait, set down beside the field she had just bought.",
    '31:20': "The hands appear again, this time reaching out. What they made is where the poor are supplied from.",
    '31:21': "Not afraid of the snow. The confidence is specific, and it rests on work finished in a warmer month.",
    '31:22': "Silk and purple. She is allowed to be one of the people her own labour clothes.",
    '31:23': "Her husband's standing in the gate is listed among her results. His reputation appears inside her portrait.",
    '31:24': "She makes fine linen and sells it. The verse says without fuss that she has customers and not merely a household.",
    '31:25': "Strength and honour are worn rather than won, and the time to come is something she rejoices at instead of dreading.",
    '31:26': "\"The law of kindness.\" Her speech is the first thing in the whole portrait to be called teaching.",
    '31:27': "\"Eateth not the bread of idleness.\" Idleness is described as something you consume, and she declines the meal.",
    '31:28': "The children rise up and the husband joins them. The praise is said out loud, by the people she has been feeding.",
    '31:29': "\"Thou excellest them all.\" He stops describing her to anyone else and turns to speak to her.",
    '31:30': "Favour and beauty are dismissed by name. The single quality left standing at the end of the verse is fear of the LORD.",
    '31:31': "\"Let her own works praise her in the gates.\" The book closes asking that a woman be given publicly what she earned.",
}
